              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "I cannot book tickets or process payments directly. However, I can help you find the best options and guide you directly to where you can purchase your return ticket.\n\nSince today is Sunday, June 7, 2026, **\"next Friday\"** usually refers to either:\n* **Friday, June 12, 2026** (this coming Friday)\n* **Friday, June 19, 2026** (the Friday of the following week)\n\n### 1. Choose Your Train Options\nWhen booking from Boston to New York, Amtrak is the primary operator. You will have two main service options:\n* **Northeast Regional:** Takes about 4 hours to 4 hours and 15 minutes. It is the most budget-friendly option.\n* **Acela:** Amtrak\u2019s high-speed train. It takes about 3.5 hours and features all-business-class seating with a slightly higher price point.\n\n**Station Tip:** I highly recommend setting your departure station to **Boston South Station (BOS)** rather than Back Bay (BBY). South Station is the beginning of the line, which gives you the best chance of finding a good seat and overhead luggage space. Your destination in New York will be **Moynihan Train Hall at Penn Station (NYP)**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. How to Book Your Ticket\nTo book your return ticket directly:\n\n1. Go to the official **[Amtrak Website](https://www.amtrak.com/)**.\n2. In the search tool, select **Round Trip**.\n3. Enter your travel details:\n   * **From:** Boston, MA - South Station (BOS)\n   * **To:** New York, NY - Moynihan Train Hall (NYP)\n   * **Depart:** June 12, 2026 (or June 19, 2026)\n   * **Return:** *Enter your desired return date*\n4. Select your preferred times, choose your seating class (Coach or Business), and complete your purchase securely.\n\n*Note: Booking as early as possible is highly recommended, as Friday afternoon trains along the Northeast Corridor tend to sell out quickly and prices rise closer to the departure date.*",
